  Fix: avoid forking basename per entry; use shell parameter expansion `${entry##*/}`. Also we can skip checking entries of huge system temp dirs — the goal is to check entries of dirs we control (the issue file's parent, maybe grandparent). But simplest robust fix: use `${entry##*/}` instead of `$(basename ...)`. That removes the fork cost; glob expansion of a big dir is still some cost but much less. The T dir glob itself: globbing is done by bash in-process; with thousands of entries that's fast-ish.

